<h1 class="article-title">Renewable Energy Complex with Terminal Planned at the Port of Liepāja</h1>
<div class="block_text mt-5 mb-4">
<div class="row">
<div class="col"><div class="col-12 output"><p>To add value to electricity produced from renewable energy resources, companies from Norway, Sweden and Latvia have established the SIA “CIS Liepāja” project management team. Its aim is to undertake the necessary studies and develop a renewable energy complex with a terminal, including a hydrogen production plant, at the Port of Liepāja. The Liepāja SEZ Authority has concluded a two-year reservation agreement with SIA “CIS Liepāja”.</p><p>The project’s lead partner, Clean Industrial Solutions Holding AS, has made investment in innovative and sustainable solutions, alongside the promotion of the circular economy, its main objective. The planned renewable energy complex and terminal at the Port of Liepāja is consistent with the European Green Deal and its aim of transforming the economy and energy sector to achieve climate neutrality by 2050. Tors Arne Pedersen, head of Clean Industrial Solutions AS, emphasises that total planned investment in the production facility and terminal in the port area could reach approximately EUR 1 billion. The planned plant would have a capacity of 1,000 MW, enabling production of about 150,000 tonnes of hydrogen.</p><p>The SIA “CIS Liepāja” project envisages construction of the renewable energy complex “Power-to-X Terminal at the Port of Liepāja” in the outer harbour by 2030. Tors Arne Pedersen, head of Clean Industrial Solutions AS, says: “Changing climatic conditions influenced by CO₂ emissions mean that we must change not only people’s habits but also develop smart and sustainable businesses, introducing innovative solutions to implement the living set of initiatives embodied in the European Green Deal. This also requires changes in the energy sector, replacing fossil fuels with renewable resources. That will allow us to create a better life for future generations.”</p><p>“The future hydrogen market is enormous. For Latvia as a whole, and Liepāja in particular, this is an excellent opportunity to become the home of the first large hydrogen production facility in the European Union,” says Tors Arne Pedersen.</p><p>Liepāja SEZ CEO Uldis Hmieļevskis says: “CIS Liepāja is a project intended to help supply CO₂-neutral energy to the EU internal market. We are delighted that one of the first modern hydrogen plants is planned for the territory of the Liepāja Special Economic Zone. It fits perfectly with Fokker Next Gen N.V.’s plans to establish an assembly plant for next-generation hydrogen-powered aircraft at Liepāja Airport. These are not the only projects we are implementing together with entrepreneurs to transform port operations and industry in line with the European Green Deal.”</p><p>Construction of the renewable energy complex and terminal at the Port of Liepāja is expected to create at least 80–100 jobs for highly qualified specialists, promoting the development of innovative technologies and education programmes based on scientific research. The project will create new supply chains and expand export opportunities, increasing international visibility and strengthening the competitiveness of the city and its port.</p></div>
